# PROVEN EXAMPLE LIBRARY

Validated prompts. Note the formula in each: subject + practical-effects mechanism + action + environment + one uncanny break + the spine.

## FEMALE

### The moth-faced sorceress (Froud)
```
Still from a 1982 dark fantasy film: a pale sorceress chest-deep in a flooded marble throne room slowly peels off her own porcelain face like a wet ceramic mask — and beneath it is a second, smaller face woven entirely from sleeping grey moths that begin to wake and lift away one by one. Glycerin water beads on her cracked face-shell; her black velvet sleeves are crusted with moth-dust and crawling with live cabbage moths cable-rigged to flutter. Drowned candelabra glow under the still black water, casting trembling caustics up the veined marble columns. Her exposed second face is calm, eyes closed, as the insects unmake her. Practical animatronic face-peel mechanism, visible foam-latex edge where the mask separates from raw under-flesh. In the style of Brian Froud. 80s dark fantasy film still, anamorphic 35mm Panavision, practical foam-latex animatronics and full-body puppetry, cable-controlled creature effects, fog-machine haze and backlit smoke, deep gel lighting — cold blue key against amber rim, Albert Whitlock matte-painting horizon, soft diffusion filter, heavy Eastman color film grain, optical compositing, no CGI, slightly faded theatrical print.
```

### The unraveling empress (Hildebrandt)
```
Still from a 1984 dark fantasy film: a young child-empress in a white gown sits on a too-large ivory throne, calm and luminous, as her body comes apart at the edges — literally unraveling thread by thread into ribbons of pale light that stream backward and are devoured by a roaring wall of grey static, a hungry Nothing eating the room from the back wall forward. Her jeweled crown hovers, weightless, in the empty air where the top of her head is already gone. Ash and snow-static drift upward against gravity. Her expression is serene, accepting. The dissolving edge is achieved with optical-printer light-streaking and physical fiber-optic strands pulled from a foam bust, grain crawling like the print itself is decaying. In the style of the Brothers Hildebrandt and Michael Whelan. 80s dark fantasy film still, anamorphic 35mm Panavision, practical foam-latex animatronics and full-body puppetry, cable-controlled creature effects, fog-machine haze and backlit smoke, deep gel lighting — cold blue key against amber rim, Albert Whitlock matte-painting horizon, soft diffusion filter, heavy Eastman color film grain, optical compositing, no CGI, slightly faded theatrical print.
```

### The serpent-spine priestess (Frazetta + Vallejo)
```
Still from a 1982 dark fantasy film: a snake-cult priestess kneels over a fire-pit ritual, arms raised, her oiled bronze skin painted in white ash glyphs — and a living python is not merely coiled around her but growing directly out of her spine, the vertebrae elongating into the serpent's body so woman and snake share one nervous system, the snake's head rising over her shoulder to mirror her chant. Firelight throws their twinned shadow huge across a wall of carved cobra idols. Acolytes in horned masks ring the pit, faces lost in smoke. The spine-to-serpent transition is a full prosthetic back-piece with cable-controlled snake animatronic, scales wet with glycerin, the seam between human skin and reptile hidden in a ridge of raised foam-latex vertebrae. In the style of Frank Frazetta and Boris Vallejo. 80s dark fantasy film still, anamorphic 35mm Panavision, practical foam-latex animatronics and full-body puppetry, cable-controlled creature effects, fog-machine haze and backlit smoke, deep gel lighting — cold blue key against amber rim, Albert Whitlock matte-painting horizon, soft diffusion filter, heavy Eastman color film grain, optical compositing, no CGI, slightly faded theatrical print.
```

